  "answer": "<p>The <b>correct answer</b> is to use a version control system such as GitHub for managing the CloudFormation template. This practice provides accountability, tracks changes, and allows for code reviews, ensuring high quality and maintainability of your infrastructure code.</p><p>Choosing <i>Developers should use the CloudFormation console's built-in editor</i> is not ideal; while the console does offer some conveniences, it lacks the robust tracking, collaboration, and collaboration abilities that version control systems offer.</p><p><i>The main focus should be on regularly updating all Amazon EC2 Linux instances and those created by CloudFormation</i> refers to an operational best practice for instance security and patching, not specifically for CloudFormation template management.</p><p><i>Enhance the template by incorporating more intrinsic CloudFormation functions like Fn::Base64 and Fn::Join</i> may be helpful but is a narrow suggestion and doesn’t address the broader need for versioning, auditability, or collaboration when managing infrastructure-as-code.</p><p><b>Tip:</b> Always store infrastructure templates (like CloudFormation) in a version control repository. This enables structured changes, accountability, and supports modern DevOps workflows.</p>",
  "query": "<p>A software team is launching a new web platform that delivers discount offers to customers. They've created an AWS CloudFormation template for provisioning all infrastructure, including several Amazon Linux EC2 instances and RDS. Which of the following is the best recommended approach for managing and maintaining the CloudFormation template?</p>",
